  "response": "몸이 먼저 반응한다는 말씀이 중요하네요. 그 일이 있었던 이후로, 몸이 \"다시 당하지 않아야 한다\"고 경계를 세운 건데, 그 보호 시스템이 지금은 안부 인사를 건네는 사람에게까지 작동하고 있는 거예요. 원래는 그 한 사람에 대한 경계였을 텐데, 이제 일상 전체로 번져서 퇴근 후에도 대화를 되짚느라 잠을 못 주무시는 거죠.\n\n몸이 보호하려고 하는 건 이해가 되는데, 그 대가로 지금 많이 지치신 것 같아요. 잠을 설치는 날이 많다고 하셨는데, 밤에 대화를 되짚을 때 보통 어떤 식으로 돌아가는 편이에요?",
  "reasoning": "The client is describing how the betrayal has affected them physically and behaviorally:\n1. Body reacts first - hypervigilance, calculating how words might be used against them even when someone just asks about their well-being\n2. Ruminating on daytime conversations after work, leading to sleepless nights\n\nThis is a significant shift from \"feeling\" to \"body response\" and behavioral patterns. The client is showing me:\n- Hypervigilance (scanning for threat in neutral interactions)\n- Rumination (replaying conversations)\n- Sleep disruption\n\nI should acknowledge what they've shared, perhaps reflect the pattern (how the betrayal has created a protective system that's now running on overdrive in everyday life), and gently explore what would be helpful. I don't want to just validate or ask another question that repeats what they've said.\n\nI could:\n- Reflect the pattern: the body learned to protect, and now that protection is activating everywhere, not just in the original context\n- Gently name that this is a protective response that has become costly\n- Maybe offer a perspective or ask what they want from here\n\nI think a good move is to reflect the mechanism (the body's protective system that's now generalized) and then check in with what they want to do with this - or offer a gentle reframe that the body isn't \"broken\" but is trying to protect, while also acknowledging the cost. Then maybe a focused question about what would help.\n\nLet me keep it concise and natural.\n",
